  <h2>Why Buying Used Looks Attractive</h2>
  <p>For a facility processing nuts, pulses, or dried fruit, a <a href="/en/color-sorter/">color sorter</a> is a serious investment item. On the used market, you can find a machine of the same capacity for far below the new price. On paper, the math is simple: you tie up less capital, and the investment appears to pay for itself faster.</p>
  <p>The problem is that a color sorter is not a machine you can judge by mechanical life the way you would a screen or a belt conveyor. Cameras, LED lighting, ejector valves, and software form a precision optical system working in concert, and every one of these components quietly loses performance over time. The machine may be running — but how well it sorts is an entirely separate question.</p>

  <h2>5 Critical Risks When Buying Used</h2>

  <h3>1. The Ejector Valves May Be at the End of Their Life</h3>
  <p>Ejector valves are the parts that knock a defective kernel out of the product with a burst of air, and on a hard-working line they fire millions of times. Past a certain firing count, a valve's response time lengthens, air leaks develop, and firing precision drops. The result is rising carryover — the good product lost to the reject stream along with the defective kernels. In other words, as the machine rejects defects it starts taking good product with them, and that means product loss, and money, straight off the bottom line.</p>

  <h3>2. Cameras and LED Systems Age</h3>
  <p>Camera sensor sensitivity and LED light output fade over the years. On a machine with degraded color sensitivity, slight color defects, faint mold spots, and shade differences become invisible. More insidious still is calibration drift: the settings you dial in in the morning start slipping by the afternoon, and the operator ends up constantly wrestling with sensitivity adjustments.</p>

  <h3>3. The Software Falls Behind</h3>
  <p>The fastest progress in modern color sorting technology is happening on the software side: AI-assisted defect recognition, shape-sorting algorithms, and ready-made recipes for specific products. An older machine's hardware cannot run these new models, and manufacturers do not release updates for discontinued platforms. The ten-year-old machine you buy today is, in software terms, frozen ten years in the past.</p>

  <h3>4. Spare Parts May Be Unavailable</h3>
  <p>When a camera board, valve block, or main board fails on a discontinued model, sourcing the part can take months — or prove impossible. At that point the machine drops to scrap value. Before buying, always verify whether the model is still in production and what the stock situation is for critical parts.</p>

  <h3>5. There May Be No One Left to Service It</h3>
  <p>Buy a machine from a brand with no remaining representation in Turkey, and there is no one to call when it breaks down. The cost and lead time of bringing in a technician from abroad add up to losses that are hard to recover for a line stopped mid-season. <a href="/en/service-spare-parts/">On-site service and spare parts support</a> matters as much in a color sorting investment as the machine itself.</p>

  <h2>Pre-Purchase Checklist</h2>
  <p>If you are still evaluating a used machine, work through these steps without skipping any:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Test the machine with your own product.</strong> Run the trial not with a clean sample the seller has prepared, but with product taken from your facility, carrying its real defect rate.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure the carryover.</strong> Weigh the good product in the reject outlet. A machine may look like it is rejecting defects — put a number on how much good product it takes along with them.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask for the valve firing counter.</strong> Most machines log the ejector firing count. This counter is like an odometer: a high count is the herald of an approaching valve replacement bill.</li>
    <li><strong>Request the spare parts list and current prices in writing.</strong> Without seeing prices for cameras, LEDs, valves, and electronic boards, you cannot know the total cost.</li>
    <li><strong>Get the service commitment in writing.</strong> Who will respond, within how many hours, and at what rate? A verbal promise will not keep the line running in season.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The Economic Reality: Old European Machine or New-Generation Machine?</h2>
  <p>On the Turkish market, "used" mostly brings older European machines to mind; Sortex in particular — the Bühler Group's color sorting brand — is a respected name that has become almost generic in our industry. These machines were well engineered for their era; but a fifteen-year-old optical machine, whatever the brand, trails today's camera and software technology. When deciding, look not only at the purchase price but at the total cost of ownership:</p>
